# Code Style and Structure Guidelines

## TypeScript Standards
- Use TypeScript across all projects (mobile, web, server)
- Enable strict typing in tsconfig.json
- Avoid `any` type - use precise types instead
- Leverage type inference where it adds clarity

## Project Organization
- Organize by feature/domain
- Group related components, hooks, utilities, and styles
- Maintain clear import/export patterns
- Keep directory structure semantic and flat where possible

## Component Architecture
- Use functional components with hooks
- Implement React.memo() for static prop components
- Break down complex components into smaller, focused pieces
- Keep component files under 300 lines when possible

## Naming Conventions
- Components: PascalCase (UserProfile, BattleScreen)
- Variables/Functions: camelCase (isFetchingData, handleUserInput)
- Directories: lowercase-with-hyphens (user-profile, battle-screen)
- Types/Interfaces: PascalCase with clear suffixes (UserState, BattleProps)

## Documentation Standards
### Comments
- Preserve existing comments unless outdated
- Add clear, concise explanations for complex logic
- Include "IMPORTANT:" prefix for critical notes
- Use "NOTE:" prefix for implementation details

### Code Documentation
```typescript
/**
 * @component ComponentName
 * @description Brief description of purpose
 * 
 * @important Critical information about usage
 * @dependencies List key dependencies
 * @maintainer Notes for maintainers
 */
```

## Performance Guidelines
- Optimize render cycles
- Implement proper cleanup in useEffect
- Use appropriate memoization (useMemo, useCallback)
- Profile and optimize heavy computations

### Animation Standards
Core Requirements:
- Use React Native's Animated API
- Prefer native driver when possible
- Target 60fps for all animations
- Implement proper cleanup

Implementation:
```typescript
// Example animation setup
const animation = new Animated.Value(0);
const animationConfig = {
  useNativeDriver: true,
  duration: 300,
  easing: Animated.Easing.ease
};

// Proper cleanup pattern
useEffect(() => {
  const anim = Animated.timing(animation, animationConfig);
  return () => anim.stop();
}, []);
```

Best Practices:
- Batch animation updates
- Avoid JS thread blocking
- Implement fallback animations
- Monitor performance metrics

Resource Management:
- Clean up animations on unmount
- Pool animation resources
- Optimize memory usage
- Handle interruptions gracefully

## Cross-Reference Standards
// IMPORTANT: When working with any code section, ALWAYS review ALL "Implementation of" and "Referenced by" 
// connections to ensure full context and maintain consistency across the codebase.

### Documentation Links
- Use precise section-to-section references
- Format: @filename.mdc#Section-Name#Subsection-Name
- Example: @battle-core-mechanics.mdc#Combat-Logic#Attack-Timing

### Reference Requirements
1. Direct Connections:
   - Link specific subsections, not just files
   - Reference exact implementation details
   - Include bidirectional links where relevant

2. Reference Format:
   ```
   // Implementation of:
   // - @source-file.mdc#Section#Subsection
   
   // Referenced by:
   // - @dependent-file.mdc#Section#Subsection
   ```

3. Change Management:
   - Update all cross-references when changing section names
   - Maintain bidirectional reference integrity
   - Document reference updates in commit messages

### Example Reference Chain
Combat Logic Implementation:
```
// Implementation of:
// - @battle-core-mechanics.mdc#Combat-Logic#Attack-Timing
// - @battle-targeting-system.mdc#Combat-Sequence#Attack-Initiation

// Referenced by:
// - @battle-movement-system.mdc#Attack-Range-Behavior#Range-Updates
// - @battle-visual-feedback.mdc#Combat-Feedback#Attack-Animation
```

Remember: References should create a clear chain of related functionality that can be followed through the codebase.